---
import SummerLayout from '../../../layouts/SummerLayout.astro';
import SpokePagination from '../../../components/summer-reading/SpokePagination.astro';
import SectionOpener from '../../../components/summer-reading/SectionOpener.astro';
import PullQuote from '../../../components/summer-reading/PullQuote.astro';
import ZoneSeam from '../../../components/summer-reading/ZoneSeam.astro';
import SectionTerminator from '../../../components/summer-reading/SectionTerminator.astro';
import EndSignature from '../../../components/summer-reading/EndSignature.astro';
import TfaTermCard from '../../../components/summer-reading/TfaTermCard.astro';
import AcephalousRings from '../../../components/summer-reading/AcephalousRings.astro';
import AscentLadder from '../../../components/summer-reading/AscentLadder.astro';
import AkweteChevron from '../../../components/summer-reading/AkweteChevron.astro';
import UliDotCluster from '../../../components/summer-reading/UliDotCluster.astro';
---

<SummerLayout title="Igbo Society and Cosmology — Things Fall Apart" section="tfa" spoke="culture">

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SPOKE HEADER — umber band + Akwete chevron beneath
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <div class="tfa-spoke-header">
    <div class="tfa-spoke-header-inner">
      <span class="tfa-spoke-header-eyebrow">Things Fall Apart · The World of the Novel</span>
      <h1 class="tfa-spoke-header-title">Igbo Society and Cosmology</h1>
      <p class="tfa-spoke-header-deck">A republic without a king, a meritocracy of titles, and a cosmos that runs from a distant creator down to the spirit assigned at a person's birth — the internal logic that makes Umuofia make sense.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="tfa-header-chevron" aria-hidden="true">
    <AkweteChevron width={1200} height={9} />
  </div>

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 1a — The Republic Without a King
       sticky nested-rings diagram · cream
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="tfa-zone--s1" data-screen-label="§1a The Political Structure">
    <div class="tfa-s1-intro">
      <SectionOpener text="tfa" eyebrow="THE POLITICAL STRUCTURE" heading="The Republic Without a King" background="cream" />
      <div class="sr-beat-card tfa-s1-intro-card">
        <p>The single most important fact about pre-colonial Igbo political life is that the Igbo had <em>no kings</em>. Scholars call the system <em>acephalous</em> — literally, "without a head." Each village was a sovereign unit. Above the family stood no higher individual; above the village council, no higher institution but the ancestral spirits and the Oracle.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tfa-s1-cols">
      <div class="tfa-s1-sticky" aria-hidden="true">
        <AcephalousRings />
      </div>
      <div class="tfa-s1-steps">
        <div class="tfa-s1-step" data-beat data-activates="umunna">
          <TfaTermCard term="Umunna" gloss="level one · the family — a patrilineage of related families">
            <p>Led by the <em>Okpara</em>, the eldest male of the senior lineage.</p>
          </TfaTermCard>
        </div>
        <div class="tfa-s1-step" data-beat data-activates="village">
          <TfaTermCard term="Oha-na-Eze" gloss="level two · the village assembly of all family heads">
            <p>Direct democracy: any adult male may speak, and decisions require consensus.</p>
          </TfaTermCard>
        </div>
        <div class="tfa-s1-step" data-beat data-activates="agegrades">
          <TfaTermCard term="Age grades" gloss="peer cohorts born within a few years of each other">
            <p>Bonds that cut <em>across</em> lineage — collective labor, policing, defense.</p>
          </TfaTermCard>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- within-section breath: diagram → levels up close -->
  <div class="tfa-breath tfa-breath--label" aria-hidden="true">
    <UliDotCluster width={54} />
    <span class="tfa-breath-text">The structure above · the levels, up close</span>
  </div>

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 1b — The levels held still + Ọfọ object study · cream
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="sr-scrolly-zone sr-scrolly-zone--ground" data-field="ground" data-tempo="still" data-ground="cream" data-text="tfa" data-screen-label="§1b The levels, up close">
    <div class="sr-zone-ground-content">

      <div class="tfa-term-cluster">
        <TfaTermCard term="Umunna" gloss="level one · the family — a patrilineage of related families descended from a common ancestor">
          <p>The basic unit of political and social life, led by the <em>Okpara</em> — the eldest male of the senior lineage. Within the Umunna, mutual support was not optional; it was the structural foundation everything else rested on.</p>
        </TfaTermCard>

        <TfaTermCard term="Oha-na-Eze" gloss="level two · the council of all family heads; the village assembly">
          <p>The highest human authority — direct democracy in which any adult male could speak and decisions required consensus. No speaker, no president, no individual with final authority. The community itself held it.</p>
        </TfaTermCard>

        <TfaTermCard term="Age grades" gloss="cohorts of men born within a few years of each other">
          <p>Cutting across family and lineage, age grades performed collective labor, civic maintenance, policing, and defense — overlapping bonds of peer solidarity that supplemented loyalty to kin and gave the community multiple structures of mutual obligation.</p>
        </TfaTermCard>
      </div>

      <div class="tfa-object-study">
        <span class="tfa-object-eyebrow">The authority behind the levels</span>
        <h3 class="tfa-object-term">Ọfọ</h3>
        <p class="tfa-object-gloss">a ritual object of ancestral authority, held by the eldest male of a lineage — the material presence of the lineage's accumulated dead</p>
        <div class="tfa-object-pair">
          <figure>
            <img
              src="/images/tfa/ofo-anam-wood.png"
              alt="Ofo anam: a wooden ritual object with a carved human face and a broad disc-shaped head, the shaft bound with iron strips and ending in iron blades."
              loading="lazy"
            />
            <figcaption><span class="tfa-held-fig">01</span>Composite ritual object (<em>ofo anam</em>); wood with a human face, decorated with strips of iron, terminating at both ends in iron blades — large at top, small at base. © The Trustees of the British Museum, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure>
            <img
              src="/images/tfa/ofo-brass.png"
              alt="A brass ofo: a cylindrical cast object with anthropomorphic features, four supports, and elaborate geometric decoration."
              loading="lazy"
            />
            <figcaption><span class="tfa-held-fig">02</span>Ritual object (<em>ofo</em>); cast in brass in cylindrical form with four supports. Anthropomorphic features and elaborate geometric decoration; remains of an iron rod through the centre are evident at the base. © The Trustees of the British Museum, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <div class="tfa-object-body">
          <p>Neither object is a symbol of power so much as a manifestation of it. The Ọfọ is not decorative; when the Okpara speaks with it in his hand, he speaks with the voice of the lineage's dead. A man who holds the Ọfọ and speaks falsely brings the ancestors' wrath upon himself. In a republic with no king, legitimacy is never inherited or enthroned. It is carried, carved, and conferred by the community and its ancestors.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <SectionTerminator text="tfa" />
  <ZoneSeam text="tfa" groundFrom="cream" groundTo="tinted" />

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 2a — The Title System
       vertical ascent ladder (panning) · tinted
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="tfa-zone--s2" data-screen-label="§2a The Title System">
    <div class="tfa-s1-intro">
      <SectionOpener text="tfa" eyebrow="ACHIEVEMENT AND HONOR" heading="The Title System" background="tinted" />
      <div class="sr-beat-card tfa-s1-intro-card">
        <p>The highest honors in Igbo society were never inherited — they were <em>earned</em> and <em>taken</em>. A man climbed a ladder of titles, each bought with accumulated wealth and a feast given back to the whole community, each conferring greater standing and heavier obligation. At the summit stood the <em>Ọzọ</em> title, reached by only a few men in any generation.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tfa-s1-cols">
      <div class="tfa-s1-sticky" aria-hidden="true">
        <AscentLadder />
      </div>
      <div class="tfa-s1-steps">
        <div class="tfa-s1-step" data-beat data-activates="t1">
          <TfaTermCard term="Taking a title" gloss="the first rung — wealth proven, a feast given to the village">
            <p>Open to any man who could afford it. A title was bought not with money alone but with a public feast that redistributed his wealth back to the community.</p>
          </TfaTermCard>
        </div>
        <div class="tfa-s1-step" data-beat data-activates="t2">
          <TfaTermCard term="Climbing higher" gloss="each title costlier than the last">
            <p>The higher a man climbed, the greater his standing — and the heavier his obligations. Title-holders hosted feasts and supported the poor, proving wealth through generosity, not accumulation alone.</p>
          </TfaTermCard>
        </div>
        <div class="tfa-s1-step" data-beat data-activates="ozo">
          <TfaTermCard term="Ọzọ" gloss="the summit — the highest individual honor, reached by only a few">
            <p>The Ọzọ title-holder presided over councils, adjudicated disputes, and spoke with recognized authority. It could not be inherited — every man began at the bottom and climbed on his own.</p>
          </TfaTermCard>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- within-section breath: the climb → what a title meant -->
  <div class="tfa-breath tfa-breath--label tfa-breath--tinted" aria-hidden="true">
    <UliDotCluster width={54} />
    <span class="tfa-breath-text">The climb above · what a title meant</span>
  </div>

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 2b — What a title meant + Ọzọ stool · still · tinted
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="sr-scrolly-zone sr-scrolly-zone--ground" data-field="ground" data-tempo="still" data-ground="tinted" data-text="tfa" data-screen-label="§2b What a title meant">
    <div class="sr-zone-ground-content">

      <div class="tfa-still">
        <div class="sr-prose-block sr-prose-block--center">
          <p>Because titles were earned and never inherited, a man's starting point counted for nothing. Okonkwo's father Unoka took no titles, grew no yams, and died in debt. In a hereditary system, Okonkwo would have inherited that diminished status. In Igbo society he began from nothing and made himself great — which is exactly what drives his consuming ambition and his consuming terror of failure. The title system that rewards him also produces his pathology: a man who cannot allow himself to look like his father, in any circumstance, at any cost.</p>
        </div>

        <PullQuote
          text="tfa"
          quote="Age was respected among his people, but achievement was revered. As the elders said, if a child washed his hands he could eat with kings."
          attribution="— Things Fall Apart, Chinua Achebe"
        />

        <div class="sr-prose-block sr-prose-block--center">
          <p>This meritocratic drive is the direct answer to a literature that imagined Africa without achievement or aspiration. Okonkwo's ambition — raw, consuming, recognizable — is the title system in action, and Achebe places a man of such recognizable human drives at the novel's center to make the point impossible to miss.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="tfa-object-study">
        <span class="tfa-object-eyebrow">The honor made material</span>
        <h3 class="tfa-object-term">An Ọzọ title-holder's stool</h3>
        <p class="tfa-object-gloss">a titled man's customized seat — the standing he climbed to, made to sit beneath him at every council and public gathering</p>
        <div class="tfa-object-single">
          <figure>
            <img
              src="/images/tfa/ozo-stool.png"
              alt="A two-tier carved wooden Igbo prestige stool with registers of concentric geometric patterns and standing animal figures around its supports, the surfaces painted in ochre, white, and dark pigments."
              loading="lazy"
            />
            <figcaption><span class="tfa-held-fig">FIG 01</span>Prestige stool of an Ọzọ title-holder, Igbo peoples. Its three registers of concentric geometric and animal motifs mark the seat of one of the most highly regarded members of Igbo society; the incisions on its base echo the <em>ichi</em> marks once worn on the faces of all ọzọ. The Metropolitan Museum of Art.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
        <div class="tfa-object-body">
          <p>To "take ọzọ" once required far more than a payment. A man underwent a yearlong passage of symbolic death, purification, and rebirth, punctuated by lavish communal feasting at his own expense. The reward was a seat like this one — a customized stool that referenced his status whenever he sat in council. In a republic with no king, authority is never enthroned; it is climbed toward, conferred by the community, and carried in a man's body, his <em>ichi</em> marks, and the seat he has earned the right to own.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <SectionTerminator text="tfa" />
  <ZoneSeam text="tfa" groundFrom="tinted" groundTo="cream" />

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 3a — The Cosmological World
       center-channel spirit-axis diagram · cream
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="sr-scrolly-zone sr-scrolly-zone--map tfa-zone--s3a" data-field="map" data-tempo="driving" data-ground="cream" data-text="tfa" data-screen-label="§3a The Cosmological World">

    <div class="tfa-s3-intro">
      <SectionOpener text="tfa" eyebrow="GODS, SPIRITS, AND FORCES" heading="The Cosmological World" background="cream" />
      <div class="sr-beat-card tfa-s3-intro-card">
        <p>Igbo religion is not a set of beliefs held separately from political and social life. It is the operating system of those lives — the framework within which governance, justice, agriculture, family, and individual identity all function. To understand why Okonkwo is exiled, why the Week of Peace matters, why the egwugwu's authority is absolute, you have to understand the cosmological structure that makes those things make sense.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="sr-zone-field sr-zone-map-field" aria-hidden="true">
      <svg
        viewBox="0 0 440 660"
        xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
        preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"
        class="sr-cosmo-axis"
        role="img"
        aria-label="A vertical cosmogram of the Igbo spiritual world: a radiant Chukwu at the summit, descending through the earth-goddess Ala to the personal chi, and branching into the egwugwu ancestral court and the Oracle"
      >
        <!-- CHUKWU — radiant supreme source -->
        <g data-show-from="chukwu">
          <g class="cw-rays">
            <line x1="220" y1="20"  x2="220" y2="38"  />
            <line x1="278" y1="34"  x2="268" y2="50"  />
            <line x1="162" y1="34"  x2="172" y2="50"  />
            <line x1="300" y1="84"  x2="284" y2="91"  />
            <line x1="140" y1="84"  x2="156" y2="91"  />
          </g>
          <circle cx="220" cy="92" r="50" class="cw-aura cw-aura--far" />
          <circle cx="220" cy="92" r="32" class="cw-aura" />
          <circle cx="220" cy="92" r="11" class="cw-core" />
          <text x="220" y="170" text-anchor="middle" class="cw-lbl cw-lbl--accent cw-lbl--lg">CHUKWU</text>
          <text x="220" y="189" text-anchor="middle" class="cw-sub">the supreme source — distant, abstract</text>
        </g>

        <!-- ALA — the grounding earth -->
        <g data-show-from="ala">
          <line x1="220" y1="212" x2="220" y2="294" class="cw-axis" />
          <line x1="100" y1="318" x2="340" y2="318" class="cw-earth" />
          <circle cx="220" cy="318" r="26" class="cw-node-ala" />
          <text x="220" y="372" text-anchor="middle" class="cw-lbl cw-lbl--accent">ALA</text>
          <text x="220" y="391" text-anchor="middle" class="cw-sub">the earth · the moral ground</text>
        </g>

        <!-- chi — the personal spirit + its companion satellite -->
        <g data-show-from="chi">
          <line x1="220" y1="344" x2="220" y2="438" class="cw-axis" />
          <line x1="232" y1="448" x2="258" y2="436" class="cw-chi-tie" />
          <circle cx="220" cy="454" r="14" class="cw-node-chi" />
          <circle cx="264" cy="432" r="5.5" class="cw-chi-sat" />
          <text x="220" y="500" text-anchor="middle" class="cw-lbl cw-lbl--chi">chi</text>
          <text x="220" y="519" text-anchor="middle" class="cw-sub cw-sub--it">the spirit assigned at birth</text>
        </g>

        <!-- Egwugwu + Oracle — the institutional gateways -->
        <g data-show-from="egwugwu">
          <line x1="208" y1="462" x2="152" y2="558" class="cw-branch" />
          <line x1="232" y1="462" x2="288" y2="558" class="cw-branch" />
          <path d="M122 616 L122 584 Q122 562 152 562 Q182 562 182 584 L182 616 Z" class="cw-gate" />
          <text x="152" y="638" text-anchor="middle" class="cw-lbl cw-lbl--sm">EGWUGWU</text>
          <text x="152" y="653" text-anchor="middle" class="cw-sub cw-sub--sm">ancestral court</text>
          <path d="M258 616 L258 584 Q258 562 288 562 Q318 562 318 584 L318 616 Z" class="cw-gate" />
          <text x="288" y="638" text-anchor="middle" class="cw-lbl cw-lbl--sm">ORACLE</text>
          <text x="288" y="653" text-anchor="middle" class="cw-sub cw-sub--sm">Agbala</text>
        </g>
      </svg>
    </div>

    <div class="sr-zone-beats">
      <div class="sr-zone-beat sr-zone-beat--left sr-zone-beat--prose" data-beat data-activates="chukwu">
        <div class="sr-beat-card">
          <p>The descent begins at the top — with the supreme source from which everything else derives, distant and abstract.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="sr-zone-beat sr-zone-beat--right sr-zone-beat--prose" data-beat data-activates="ala">
        <div class="sr-beat-card">
          <p>From the cosmic to the immediate: the earth, and the moral force that governs what the community may and may not do.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="sr-zone-beat sr-zone-beat--left sr-zone-beat--prose" data-beat data-activates="chi">
        <div class="sr-beat-card">
          <p>Down to the level of the single person — the spirit assigned at birth that walks with them through life.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="sr-zone-beat sr-zone-beat--right sr-zone-beat--prose" data-beat data-activates="egwugwu">
        <div class="sr-beat-card">
          <p>And the institutions that bridge the human and the divine: the ancestral court, and the Oracle whose word is final.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

  </section>

  <!-- within-section breath: structure → depth -->
  <div class="tfa-breath tfa-breath--label" aria-hidden="true">
    <UliDotCluster width={54} />
    <span class="tfa-breath-text">Structure above · depth below</span>
  </div>

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 3b — Cosmological term cards + Ikenga · still · cream
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="sr-scrolly-zone sr-scrolly-zone--ground" data-field="ground" data-tempo="still" data-ground="cream" data-text="tfa" data-screen-label="§3b Cosmological terms (held)">
    <div class="sr-zone-ground-content">

      <div class="tfa-term-cluster">
        <TfaTermCard term="Chukwu / Chineke" gloss="the supreme creative force from which all things derive">
          <p>Distant and supreme — not directly petitioned or personally accessible. Interactions with the divine world happen primarily through lesser spirits and deities. The concept has partial analogy to monotheistic traditions, but Igbo theology does not imply a direct personal relationship with Chukwu. He is the ground of being, not a God who intervenes in daily life. Chukwu was not typically represented in figural art — the deity's distance and abstraction made material representation unsuitable. Visual and material religious life in Igboland centered on the <em>alusi</em> (lesser deities) and personal shrine figures, not on Chukwu directly.</p>
        </TfaTermCard>

        <TfaTermCard term="Ala" gloss="the earth goddess; the most immediately powerful moral force in Igbo life">
          <p>Ala governs the land, fertility, and morality. She defines what constitutes an <em>alu</em> — an abomination, a taboo violation. Crimes against Ala are the most serious offenses possible, because they violate the sacred ground on which the entire community depends for its survival. When Okonkwo accidentally kills a clansman's son and is immediately sent into exile, the punishment is not social censure — it is the community's necessary response to protect itself from Ala's wrath. The crime is against the earth itself.</p>
        </TfaTermCard>

        <TfaTermCard term="chi" gloss="the personal spirit assigned at birth that accompanies a person and shapes their destiny">
          <p>The chi is neither a deity nor an impersonal force like fate, but something between the two: the personification of what a person is capable of becoming. "When a man says yes, his chi says yes also" — personal determination and chi work in alignment. But the chi also sets limits. When Okonkwo reflects that his chi has turned against him, he is confronting how much any person can will their own fate, and how much is determined by forces beyond their choice. Achebe deliberately leaves <em>chi</em> untranslated throughout the novel — scholars have compared it to the Hindu concept of karma, the Greek <em>daimon</em>, and the Christian soul, but none of these comparisons fully capture it. By refusing to translate it, Achebe refuses to let the idea be absorbed into a simpler framework.</p>
        </TfaTermCard>

        <div class="tfa-cluster-image">
          <img
            src="/images/tfa/ikenga.png"
            alt="A carved wooden Igbo Ikenga shrine figure with two large upswept ram's horns rising from a stylized head, the body incised with rows of geometric notches, mounted on a dark display base."
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <p class="tfa-held-caption"><span class="tfa-held-fig">Fig 02</span>Shrine Figure (<em>Ikenga</em>), Igbo artist, Cross River region, 19th–20th c. Wood, camwood. Associated with male achievement and ambition; its ram's horns signify stubborn perseverance, the machete the aggression with which challenges are met. The figure makes visible the same drive that animates Okonkwo — that a man's chi and his own determination work together to shape what he becomes. The Metropolitan Museum of Art, CC0. Object ID 314139.</p>
        </div>

        <TfaTermCard term="Agbala" gloss="the Oracle of the Hills and the Caves; also an insult meaning a man without titles">
          <p>The Oracle at Awka is the supreme judicial and spiritual authority for Umuofia and the surrounding villages. Its priestess, Chielo, speaks in the god's voice; her rulings carry the weight of divine command. No community goes to war without first consulting the Oracle. When the Oracle decrees that Ikemefuna must be killed, the elders act on that decree without question. The word <em>Agbala</em> carries a double meaning: it is the name of the Oracle, and it is also the insult Okonkwo directs at men without titles — without standing, "like a woman." The Oracle is the source of ultimate authority; the insult is the accusation of ultimate failure. Both derive from the single concept of a person without standing.</p>
        </TfaTermCard>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <SectionTerminator text="tfa" />
  <ZoneSeam text="tfa" groundFrom="cream" groundTo="tinted" />

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 4 — The Egwugwu · still · tinted
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="sr-scrolly-zone sr-scrolly-zone--ground" data-field="ground" data-tempo="still" data-ground="tinted" data-text="tfa" data-screen-label="§4 The Egwugwu">
    <div class="sr-zone-ground-content">

      <SectionOpener text="tfa" eyebrow="THE ANCESTRAL COURT" heading="The Egwugwu" background="tinted" />

      <div class="tfa-still">
        <div class="sr-prose-block sr-prose-block--center">
          <p>The egwugwu are men of the village who don elaborate masks and costumes to become, for the duration of the ceremony, the ancestral spirits of the dead. The grammar here is precise: they do not represent or perform the ancestors — they become them. When masked, the man inside is gone; only the spirit is present. Women are forbidden from approaching the egwugwu's compound or knowing what lies behind the masks.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tfa-image-pair">
          <img
            src="/images/tfa/ekpe-masquerade-group.jpg"
            alt="Five Ekpe masquerade figures standing in an outdoor clearing in southeastern Nigeria, wearing elaborate fiber costumes and painted masks. Photographed by G.I. Jones, c. 1930s."
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <img
            src="/images/tfa/ekpe-masquerade-figure.jpg"
            alt="Close-up portrait of an Ekpe masquerade figure wearing a painted white mask with spotted markings and a fiber costume, holding a wand. Photographed by G.I. Jones, c. 1930s."
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </div>
        <p class="tfa-pair-caption">Ekpe masquerade figures photographed by G.I. Jones in southeastern Nigeria, c. 1930–1939 — year-end harvest celebrations honoring ancestors and the earth. In the ceremony, the masked figure is not a man in costume but the ancestral presence itself. © Museum of Archaeology and Anthropology, University of Cambridge.</p>

        <div class="sr-prose-block sr-prose-block--center">
          <p>The egwugwu constitute the highest court in the land. They hear disputes between families and clans that the village council cannot resolve. Their rulings carry the authority of the ancestral dead — the entire accumulated community. When Enoch tears the mask from an egwugwu in the novel's third section, he does not commit an insult or a cultural violation. He kills an ancestor. The egwugwu's response — burning Enoch's compound and the church — follows directly from the logic of what has occurred: a death requires a destructive response in turn.</p>
        </div>

        <PullQuote text="tfa" attribution="— Things Fall Apart, Chinua Achebe">
          <p>"Okonkwo's wives, and perhaps other women as well, might have noticed that the second egwugwu had the springy walk of Okonkwo. And they might have noticed that Okonkwo was not among the titled men and elders who sat behind the row of egwugwu. But if they thought these things they kept them within themselves."</p>
        </PullQuote>

        <div class="sr-prose-block sr-prose-block--center">
          <p>The passage above is one of the novel's most carefully managed moments. Achebe allows the reader to see what the women may have noticed — the springy walk, the absence of Okonkwo from his usual place — while also showing that the women keep what they know "within themselves." The egwugwu's power depends on the community's collective maintenance of its necessary fiction. Everyone, at some level, knows what lies behind the masks. The institution functions because everyone agrees to make it function. Colonial disruption of the egwugwu is not simply the tearing away of a costume; it is the destruction of the community's most important shared agreement about how authority works.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <SectionTerminator text="tfa" />
  <ZoneSeam text="tfa" groundFrom="tinted" groundTo="cream" />

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 5 — Key Practices · uli ideogram triptych · cream
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="sr-scrolly-zone sr-scrolly-zone--ground" data-field="ground" data-tempo="still" data-ground="cream" data-text="tfa" data-screen-label="§5 Key Practices">
    <div class="sr-zone-ground-content">

      <SectionOpener text="tfa" eyebrow="SOCIAL STRUCTURE" heading="Key Practices" background="cream" />

      <div class="tfa-still">
        <div class="sr-prose-block sr-prose-block--center">
          <p>Three practices in the novel require contextual explanation to be understood from the inside: the Week of Peace, the bride price, and the <em>osu</em> system. Each one reflects the Igbo world's internal coherence — its logic, its values, and its genuine moral complexity — more clearly than any summary of Igbo culture can.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="tfa-triptych" role="group" aria-label="Three key practices of Igbo life">
        <div class="tfa-triptych-head">
          <span>Ọmenala · the moral order</span>
          <span class="is-accent">three practices, one ground</span>
        </div>
        <div class="tfa-triptych-cols">

          <!-- Izu Eke — the Week of Peace -->
          <div class="tfa-tri-col" data-practice="izu-eke">
            <div class="tfa-tri-glyph" aria-hidden="true">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 76 78" fill="none">
                <circle cx="38" cy="40" r="26" fill="none" stroke="#C4722A" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="3.5 4" opacity="0.5" />
                <path d="M12 40 A26 26 0 0 1 64 40" fill="none" stroke="#C4722A" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linecap="round" />
                <circle cx="38" cy="14" r="3"   fill="#C4722A" />
                <circle cx="64" cy="40" r="2.6" fill="#C4722A" />
                <circle cx="38" cy="66" r="2.6" fill="#C4722A" />
                <circle cx="12" cy="40" r="2.6" fill="#C4722A" />
                <path d="M38 51 C 33.5 46, 33.5 41, 38 37 C 42.5 41, 42.5 46, 38 51 Z" fill="none" stroke="#C4722A" stroke-width="1.4" opacity="0.9" />
                <line x1="38" y1="51" x2="38" y2="44" stroke="#C4722A" stroke-width="1.4" />
              </svg>
            </div>
            <h3 class="tfa-tri-term">Izu Eke</h3>
            <div class="tfa-tri-gloss">sacred time</div>
            <p class="tfa-tri-essence">Before planting, Ala forbids all conflict — the earth must not be shown anger before it is asked to grow.</p>
            <button type="button" class="tfa-tri-more" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="detail-izu-eke">
              <span>Read the full account</span><span class="tfa-tri-more-mark" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
            </button>
            <div class="tfa-tri-detail" id="detail-izu-eke" role="note" aria-label="Izu Eke — the full account">
              <button type="button" class="tfa-tri-detail-close" aria-label="Close">×</button>
              <div class="tfa-tri-detail-body">
                <div class="tfa-tri-detail-head">
                  <span class="tfa-tri-detail-term">Izu Eke</span>
                  <span class="tfa-tri-detail-gloss">the Week of Peace — the sacred week before planting during which Ala prohibits all conflict</span>
                </div>
                <p>During the Week of Peace, no one in Umuofia is to speak harshly, let alone raise a hand. The earth must not be shown anger before it is asked to grow. When Okonkwo beats his wife Ojiugo during the Week of Peace, the community's alarm has nothing to do with the violence per se. Okonkwo has offended Ala at the moment she is supposed to be welcoming the new season. The entire community's harvest is at risk. This is one of the novel's early and crucial signals that in Igbo society, individual behavior and communal welfare are inseparable — mediated not through social pressure alone but through the cosmological framework.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- Ika-Akalika — the bride price -->
          <div class="tfa-tri-col" data-practice="ika-akalika">
            <div class="tfa-tri-glyph" aria-hidden="true">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 76 70" fill="none">
                <g fill="#C4722A">
                  <circle cx="13" cy="30" r="2.6" />
                  <circle cx="22" cy="23" r="2.6" />
                  <circle cx="22" cy="37" r="2.6" />
                </g>
                <g fill="#C4722A">
                  <circle cx="63" cy="30" r="2.6" />
                  <circle cx="54" cy="23" r="2.6" />
                  <circle cx="54" cy="37" r="2.6" />
                </g>
                <line x1="30" y1="21" x2="46" y2="39" stroke="#C4722A" stroke-width="2.3" stroke-linecap="round" />
                <line x1="46" y1="21" x2="30" y2="39" stroke="#C4722A" stroke-width="2.3" stroke-linecap="round" />
                <path d="M15 41 C 26 60, 50 60, 61 41" fill="none" stroke="#C4722A" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="3.5 4" opacity="0.7" />
              </svg>
            </div>
            <h3 class="tfa-tri-term">Ika-Akalika</h3>
            <div class="tfa-tri-gloss">the bond</div>
            <p class="tfa-tri-essence">Broomsticks exchanged between two families — a bond formed, not a bride bought.</p>
            <button type="button" class="tfa-tri-more" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="detail-ika-akalika">
              <span>Read the full account</span><span class="tfa-tri-more-mark" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
            </button>
            <div class="tfa-tri-detail" id="detail-ika-akalika" role="note" aria-label="Ika-Akalika — the full account">
              <button type="button" class="tfa-tri-detail-close" aria-label="Close">×</button>
              <div class="tfa-tri-detail-body">
                <div class="tfa-tri-detail-head">
                  <span class="tfa-tri-detail-term">Ika-Akalika</span>
                  <span class="tfa-tri-detail-gloss">the bride price — the negotiated exchange between families that formalizes a marriage</span>
                </div>
                <p>Conducted through a ritual exchange of short broomsticks — avoiding the vulgarity of naming a price for a person's daughter. The bride price is not the purchase of a woman; it is the establishment of a bond between families and the definition of each side's obligations. As rendered in the novel during Obierika's daughter's suitor visit, its negotiation is elaborate, ceremonial, and meaningful — a social form in which the families feel each other out across the broomsticks, and the final arrangement reflects not just economics but the relative standing of the families and the character of the match.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- osu — the dedicated and excluded -->
          <div class="tfa-tri-col" data-practice="osu">
            <div class="tfa-tri-glyph tfa-tri-glyph--tall" aria-hidden="true">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 76 104" fill="none">
                <circle cx="25" cy="34" r="20" fill="none" stroke="#C4722A" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="3.5 4" opacity="0.5" />
                <g fill="#6B6355">
                  <circle cx="19" cy="30" r="2.1" />
                  <circle cx="29" cy="27" r="2.1" />
                  <circle cx="23" cy="40" r="2.1" />
                  <circle cx="33" cy="38" r="2.1" />
                </g>
                <line x1="51" y1="6"  x2="51" y2="99" stroke="#2C2820" stroke-width="1.3" stroke-dasharray="2 4" opacity="0.55" />
                <path d="M64 8 L70 18 L58 18 Z" fill="none" stroke="#C4722A" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linejoin="round" />
                <line x1="64" y1="18" x2="64" y2="30" stroke="#C4722A" stroke-width="1.3" stroke-dasharray="2 3" />
                <line x1="64" y1="32" x2="64" y2="92" stroke="#C4722A" stroke-width="1.6" />
                <circle cx="64" cy="34" r="3" fill="#C4722A" />
                <circle cx="64" cy="63" r="3" fill="#C4722A" />
                <circle cx="64" cy="92" r="3" fill="#C4722A" />
              </svg>
            </div>
            <h3 class="tfa-tri-term">osu</h3>
            <div class="tfa-tri-gloss">the boundary</div>
            <p class="tfa-tri-essence">Dedicated to a deity and set permanently outside the community — a status passed down, unchosen.</p>
            <button type="button" class="tfa-tri-more" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="detail-osu">
              <span>Read the full account</span><span class="tfa-tri-more-mark" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
            </button>
            <div class="tfa-tri-detail" id="detail-osu" role="note" aria-label="osu — the full account">
              <button type="button" class="tfa-tri-detail-close" aria-label="Close">×</button>
              <div class="tfa-tri-detail-body">
                <div class="tfa-tri-detail-head">
                  <span class="tfa-tri-detail-term">osu</span>
                  <span class="tfa-tri-detail-gloss">a person dedicated to the service of a deity — permanently excluded from the free community</span>
                </div>
                <p>The <em>osu</em> are people dedicated to a particular deity, or those whose ancestors were so dedicated. Their status is permanent and hereditary; they cannot marry freeborn Igbo, cannot take titles, and are excluded from communal activities.</p>
                <p>How a person became <em>osu</em> varied. Some were purchased as slaves and dedicated to a deity — either to atone for a serious crime committed by their community or to seek the deity's assistance in a crisis. When a community member committed a grave offense against Ala, the community could also dedicate that person to the goddess to forestall her wrath. Some people voluntarily sought asylum at a shrine during the transatlantic slave trade era, accepting <em>osu</em> status to avoid being sold. Others were born into it: once a person was <em>osu</em>, their children and all subsequent descendants were <em>osu</em> as well. The original act of dedication — however it occurred, however many generations back — was permanent and hereditary.</p>
                <p>When Christianity arrives and Nwoye's fellow converts include <em>osu</em>, the question of whether freeborn Christians and <em>osu</em> Christians can worship together becomes a genuine crisis. The church's willingness to include the <em>osu</em> is one of the real reasons it attracts Igbo people whom the traditional system has marginalized — and one of the reasons Okonkwo and the elders find the church threatening. The <em>osu</em> system is one of the novel's clearest illustrations of Achebe's moral complexity: a society can possess genuine philosophical depth and genuine moral failure simultaneously.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="tfa-still">
        <div class="sr-prose-block sr-prose-block--center sr-prose-block--lede">
          <p>The Igbo world that Achebe renders is not a simple or idealized world. It contains the <em>osu</em> system, the exile of twins, the killing of Ikemefuna, the subordination of women. Achebe does not sanitize any of this. What he insists on is that this world has internal coherence — that its practices, however difficult, follow from a consistent set of values and cosmological commitments. Understanding this coherence is not the same as endorsing every practice it generates. But it is the prerequisite for understanding Achebe's fundamental assertion: that pre-colonial Igboland was a world with philosophical depth, political sophistication, and genuine human achievement — not a darkness awaiting European light.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <EndSignature text="tfa" />

    </div>
  </section>

